  <!-- Intro -->
  <p>
    I spent five weeks here alone. On 28 July, at eight in the morning, the plane from Dubai lands in Brisbane, carrying the three people who mean everything to me: Lucy, Joris, Linnea. This article tells the story of our first days as a family in Australia. The reunion, the traffic jam right after, the jet lag nights, the move into the house sit. With all the highs and all the lows.
  </p>

  <!-- Key takeaways -->
  <div class="info-box">
    <p class="info-box__titel">Key takeaways</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The flights for Lucy and the kids cost 3,061.80 euros in total (Emirates, Düsseldorf via Dubai to Brisbane).</li>
      <li>Luggage trick: on our fare, 30 kg per person, split across as many pieces as you like. We packed 7 lightweight duffel bags instead of suitcases.</li>
      <li>Plan the arrival day generously: for us, almost two hours of accident traffic came on top of the two-hour drive to Byron Bay.</li>
      <li>Jet lag with kids: pushing through the first day helps, but it still took several days until everyone slept through the night.</li>
      <li>House sitting right after arrival takes enormous pressure off: four weeks without rent, with a house, garden and animals.</li>
      <li>Winter is a plus, not a minus: chilly nights, around 20 degrees in the daytime, and the kids are outside every single day.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Section: monitor -->
  <h2 id="monitor">Two screens in Brisbane</h2>

  <p>I was almost late. That morning the drive to Brisbane took me nearly three and a half hours, rush hour, heavy traffic. The whole way, the same thought kept looping: the three of them will walk out of that plane any minute, they have no Australian phone reception, and I am not there. Luckily the flight itself was delayed. It was enough.</p>

  <p>In the arrivals area in Brisbane there are two screens above the waiting crowd showing who is currently walking out of the baggage hall towards the exit. So you can already see in advance when the people you are waiting for are about to come through the door.</p>

  <figure class="artikel-figur">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-brisbane-ankunft-monitore.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img src="/assets/images/au-brisbane-ankunft-monitore.jpg" alt="Arrivals area at Brisbane Airport: two monitors under the ceiling show the camera feed of the corridor that arriving passengers walk through" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1200" height="675" />
    </picture>
    <figcaption>The two screens above the arrivals gate. This is where I saw the three of them first.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>I stared at the screen the whole time. And then, there they were, as a small camera image: Joris pushing the stroller with Linnea asleep inside, Lucy pushing the luggage trolley with the seven heavy duffel bags. The grin came instantly. When the three of them came around the corner in real life, the tears came too. We did not say much and simply held each other. Joris meanwhile was looking around with huge eyes, because everything was new. Linnea slept through the entire moment.</p>

