  <p>Why the three of them flew four weeks after me in the first place, and what five weeks alone on the other side of the world feel like, is in the article about <a href="/en/arrived-first-days-in-australia">my first days alone in Australia</a>. The same weeks also exist in moving pictures: as the first episode of our documentary on YouTube, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UJRxrk_UyIw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">"Angekommen. Und es fühlt sich falsch an."</a> (in German). The short version: those five weeks made one thing very clear to me. Life without my family is no longer fulfilling. No matter where you are and how beautiful it is.</p>

  <!-- Section: traffic jam -->
  <h2 id="stau">A reunion in a traffic jam</h2>

  <p>The luggage trick first, because after this nobody thinks about logistics anymore: on our <a href="https://www.emirates.com/de/german/before-you-fly/baggage/checked-baggage/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Emirates</a> fare, every person could take 30 kilograms of luggage, split across as many pieces as you like. So we did not buy suitcases, we bought duffel bags. They weigh next to nothing and they are flexible. In the end, seven olive-green bags stood on the trolley: our entire remaining life from Germany. The flights for the three of them cost 3,061.80 euros in total. How we booked and why the price was what it was is in the <a href="/en/cost-of-flights-to-australia-family-guide">article about flight costs for a family</a>.</p>

  <figure class="artikel-figur">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-kofferraum-seesaecke.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img src="/assets/images/au-kofferraum-seesaecke.jpg" alt="Fully packed boot of the Jeep: olive-green duffel bags with Emirates tags, a green carry-on suitcase and a folded stroller" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1200" height="675" />
    </picture>
    <figcaption>Seven duffel bags, one stroller, one boot. It fits. Just.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>Then the drive home, two hours to Byron Bay. So we thought. Because of an accident, nothing moved on the highway for almost two hours. Linnea had woken up right before we hit the jam, of all moments. I had imagined that moment differently. I could not pick her up, since I was at the wheel. I caught up on it in the middle of the traffic jam, between standing cars. She was completely overtired and still incredibly happy.</p>

  <Randnotiz lang="en" ausrichtung="rechts">Joris' first verdict on Australia from the back seat: the trucks look different. Apart from that, the highway looks pretty normal. Except for the wrong side.</Randnotiz>

  <p>Lucy was completely wrecked. She had not slept a minute on the plane, probably that subconscious tension of being solely responsible for two kids on a long-haul flight. Joris practically fell asleep standing up when we got out of the car in the jam, but he powered through because he did not want to miss anything. And so there we stood, on an Australian highway, finally a family of four again, talking about the flight and about what we wanted to do in the first days.</p>

  <!-- Section: beach -->
  <h2 id="strand">The first beach afternoon</h2>

  <p>At the Airbnb in Byron Bay we quickly unloaded the car, and then we went straight to the beach. It was the moment of the day, maybe of the week: both kids were suddenly wide awake the second they stood at the water. Joris ran back and forth through the shallow water like mad, Linnea jumped into the small shallow waves at the front. The tiredness of 24 hours of travelling had simply evaporated.</p>

  <PullQuote lang="en">Simply seeing that we are doing the right thing, and that despite all the hard steps still ahead of us, we are somehow making this life here work.</PullQuote>

  <p>That is how I described this afternoon afterwards, and I stand by it. The most important thing is that the kids live a happy and fulfilled life. On this afternoon, it looked exactly like that.</p>

