    </picture>
    <figcaption>Day 2, edge of the dunes, all four of us in one picture. The first one in five weeks.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <!-- Section: jet lag -->
  <h2 id="jetlag">Jet lag times four: the first nights</h2>

  <p>How long does jet lag last with kids? For us: several days until everyone slept through the night again. The three of them landed in the morning, and we deliberately pushed through the day to get into the rhythm. It helped, but it was no magic trick.</p>

  <AufklappBox titel="Our jet lag days in detail" lang="en">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Push through the day:</strong> morning arrival, no nap for the big ones, early bedtime. The classic, and it works, just not instantly.</li>
      <li><strong>Early nights:</strong> for the first days, Lucy fell asleep with the kids between 6 and 7 pm. Not planned, it just happened.</li>
      <li><strong>Relapses allowed:</strong> Linnea went back to daytime naps for a few days, which she normally does not do anymore. The mix of jet lag and all the new impressions was clearly exhausting, Joris was unusually tired early too.</li>
      <li><strong>Early risers win:</strong> we get up early and go to bed early anyway. The Australian rhythm plays right into that. Hence the sunrise beach photos on the second morning.</li>
    </ul>
  </AufklappBox>

  <figure class="artikel-figur">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-duenenpfad-morgen.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img src="/assets/images/au-duenenpfad-morgen.jpg" alt="Linnea in a red poncho walks alone down the sandy dune path between coastal trees towards the beach, early morning light" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1200" height="675" />
    </picture>
    <figcaption>Day 2, just after sunrise. If everyone is awake anyway, you might as well go to the beach.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <!-- Section: house sit -->
  <h2 id="housesit">Move number one: the house sit in the hinterland</h2>

  <p>The Airbnb was only ever the bridge: booked until 31 July, 765.70 euros in total for the almost ten days during which I had already lived there alone. For the three of them it was just three nights. Then the next move was due, the first as a family: out of Byron Bay, into the hinterland near Murwillumbah, into our house sit for the next four weeks.</p>

  <p>In case the term is new to you: house sitting means looking after someone's home while they are travelling. You live there for free and in return take care of the garden, the pool or, in our case, the animals. No rent, but real responsibility.</p>

  <p>The house is an absolute stroke of luck. Beautiful, big, well kept, in a very quiet suburb, with a property the kids can run onto straight from the front door. Four weeks without moving, without rent, with time to plan everything else. How house sitting works in detail and how we found our sits will get its own article, because it is our biggest cost lever while settling in. What life here costs overall, we track in our <a href="/en/cost-of-moving-to-australia-family-guide">big cost guide</a>.</p>

