  <!-- Section: everyday life -->
  <h2 id="alltag">Chickens, two dogs, passionfruit</h2>

  <p>The house sit comes with two dogs. Tinsel looks like a Dalmatian but is a mixed breed, nobody knows exactly what. She was shy and anxious for the first two or three days; since she thawed, she has been a wonderfully uncomplicated, sweet dog who listens to every word on walks. Hammer, the small black one, has only lived here for a few weeks himself, the owners rescued him. His hearing is not great anymore, but he is a very funny old gentleman who has zero interest in walks: he always trots a few steps down the driveway, turns around and shuffles back to the house.</p>

  <p>The kids feed both dogs entirely on their own, roam the property with them, and Linnea especially is head over heels. Only when it comes to poo patrol does the fun stop. That remains a parent job.</p>

  <FotoStory bilder={fotostoryHousesit} lang="en" ueberschrift="House-sit life in three pictures" />

  <p>Then there are the chickens. Once a day someone checks what has been laid: sometimes three eggs, sometimes one, sometimes none, the ladies are getting on a bit. Fresh food and water every two days, and of course the kids handle that. Passionfruit grows along the fence, and the two of them are obsessed: every day they check whether ripe fruit has dropped, ready to eat on the spot.</p>

  <Randnotiz lang="en" ausrichtung="rechts" pfeil>I always come along for egg collecting. In this region, carpet pythons do occasionally help themselves to a chicken. So far: not a single visit. Snakes are barely active in winter.</Randnotiz>

  <p>And yes, that margin note is meant seriously: the <a href="https://murwillumbahsnakecatchers.com.au/coastal-carpet-python" target="_blank" rel="noopener">local snake catcher in Murwillumbah</a> regularly reports carpet pythons helping themselves to chickens. In the Australian winter, though, snakes are barely active; things only pick up again in spring. So our rule is relaxed, but it is a rule: the kids do not go into the coop alone.</p>

  <!-- Section: bird drawing -->
  <h2 id="vogelbild">Walkie-talkies and a bird drawing</h2>

  <p>Two things tell the story of our arrival better than any summary. The first: the walkie-talkies the kids brought from Germany. The typical walk with Tinsel now looks like this: I take Linnea and one walkie-talkie. Joris sits on the couch at home and radios us the whole time. We even tested whether the range covers the whole distance, and yes: the signal held the whole way. Back in Germany we had a lovely flat, but no garden. A property where kids can simply run off was always a dream of ours. Here, it is almost the default.</p>

  <p>The second: a sheet of paper. Lucy had taken both kids and Tinsel to a small park nearby; the "playground" there consists of one swing in the middle of a huge meadow. Afterwards, Joris sat down and drew what he had seen: the birds.</p>

  <figure class="scrapbook">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-vogelbild-joris-zeichnung.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img src="/assets/images/au-vogelbild-joris-zeichnung.jpg" alt="Joris' coloured pencil drawing: a cockatoo with a yellow crest, a brush turkey with a red head, a flying bird, trees and an orange sun" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1200" height="900" />
    </picture>
    <figcaption class="scrapbook__caption">Cockatoo, brush turkey, orange sun: Australia, drawn after one week.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>In the picture: a cockatoo with a yellow crest, a brush turkey, a flying bird, plus trees and a big orange sun. Brush turkeys and cockatoos the kids can now name with confidence. And then there is their favourite: a bird that looks like it is wearing a yellow mask. The two of them find it absolutely hilarious.</p>

  <Randnotiz lang="en" ausrichtung="rechts">The bird with the yellow mask really is called that: Masked Lapwing. The yellow face wattles gave it its name.</Randnotiz>

  <!-- Section: skate parks -->
  <h2 id="skateparks">Skate parks: the new family hobby</h2>

  <p>One thing we had not planned for: besides working our way through the beaches, the first days turned into a little skate park tour. We discovered that basically every town here has its own skate park, and not a sad concrete leftover, but properly good, well-kept facilities. Free, public, simply there. Joris borrowed a park scooter from the people whose house we are sitting, we are kindly allowed to use everything, helmet included. Linnea has her toy unicorn to ride around on. And Lucy and I have since bought a skateboard and are trying to teach ourselves.</p>

  <figure class="artikel-figur">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/au-skatepark-joris.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img src="/assets/images/au-skatepark-joris.jpg" alt="Spacious concrete skate park between trees, Joris standing at the edge with helmet and scooter, other kids and teenagers in the background" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1200" height="675" />
    </picture>
    <figcaption>Rails, bowls, shade sails, picnic tables. In a small town.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>The only park that was properly busy was the one in Byron Bay. And that is exactly where the most beautiful thing was on display: every age group was there. Dads with their kids, the dads mostly on skateboards themselves, teenagers, some older folks too. And nobody gets worked up about toddlers standing in the way. Simply a lovely way of sharing a space. Exactly the kind of everyday life we came here for: kids outside, movement, and a public space that carries it.</p>

  <div class="skatepark-duo">
    <figure class="artikel-figur artikel-figur--duo">
      <picture>
        <source srcset="/assets/images/au-skatepark-linnea-einhorn-p960.webp" type="image/webp" />
        <img src="/assets/images/au-skatepark-linnea-einhorn-p960.jpg" alt="Linnea pushes her white toy unicorn across the skate park concrete against the light, a ramp next to her" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="960" height="1280" />
      </picture>
      <figcaption>Unicorn meets half-pipe.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="artikel-figur artikel-figur--duo">
      <picture>
        <source srcset="/assets/images/au-skatepark-familienselfie.webp" type="image/webp" />
        <img src="/assets/images/au-skatepark-familienselfie.jpg" alt="Tilted family selfie from a low angle at the skate park: Christian doing the shaka sign, Joris grinning, Lucy and Linnea behind, blue sky" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1200" height="900" />
      </picture>
      <figcaption>Skate park closing shot, slightly crooked. Matches our riding style.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <!-- Section: verdict -->
  <h2 id="bilanz">What remains after week one</h2>

  <p>What does the first week as an emigrated family feel like? The short answer: good, but we deliberately do not fully trust the peace yet. There is still a bit of holiday mood in the air, because I am not working yet and Joris is not in school yet. It is too early to say that all of this was worth it. Right now it feels like it. But that is also simply the first phase.</p>

  <p>For Lucy, the beginning was the harder part. Everything was a lot: the new impressions, the new life, the huge responsibility, and then we also had to move again after just a few days. Her questions were the big ones: How are we going to manage all of this? What is best for the kids? It took a few days until she had properly arrived too. There was no real argument, but not everything was easy either.</p>

  <PullQuote lang="en">The beginning is exhausting, because you cannot do anything in your usual rhythms. But it is rather beautiful when you notice, in lots of small moments, what life could be like.</PullQuote>

  <p>Added to that is Lucy's second, quieter finding: it is lovely to see that Joris and Linnea feel so at home here, and that the move does not seem nearly as hard for them as you would have expected beforehand.</p>

  <p>And winter? So far it is an argument for Australia, not against it. Nights get chilly, and with the poorly insulated houses here you really notice it. During the day the <a href="http://reg.bom.gov.au/climate/averages/tables/cw_058216.shtml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">August mean at the coast</a> is a 20.2 degree maximum and a 13.2 degree minimum, and the sun feels warmer here: as soon as it comes out, it feels like beach weather to us. In practice that means: fire in the wood stove in the evening or morning, beach or skate park in the afternoon. The mere fact that it is winter and we are still outside every single day is almost worth the whole thing on its own.</p>

  <InlineVideo
    src="/assets/images/au-kaminofen-loop.mp4"
    poster="/assets/images/au-kaminofen-poster.jpg"
    caption="Evening programme in August: lighting the fire, all four of us in front of it."
    lang="en"
    aspectRatio="16 / 9"
  />

  <p>This is how our days end at the moment: Lucy lights the wood stove with the kids, it gets dark outside, the house smells of firewood. How things continue, with school, housing and everything still open, we will tell step by step. How this whole journey started in the first place is in <a href="/en/from-vegetable-garden-to-australia-family-move">our story from vegetable garden to Australia</a>. And how we prepared the kids for all of this, in the <a href="/en/moving-to-australia-with-kids-family-guide">guide to emigrating with kids</a>.</p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

  <h3>How long does jet lag last for kids after arriving in Australia?</h3>
  <p>For us it took several days until everyone slept through the night again. We deliberately pushed through the arrival day, but Lucy still fell asleep with the kids between 6 and 7 pm at first, and Linnea went back to daytime naps for a few days.</p>

  <h3>What did the flights to Australia cost for the family?</h3>
  <p>3,061.80 euros for Lucy and the two kids together, Emirates from Düsseldorf via Dubai to Brisbane, booked in late March 2026 via Expedia. My single flight a month earlier cost 467 euros.</p>

  <h3>How much luggage can a family take on Emirates?</h3>
  <p>On our fare it was 30 kilograms per person, split across as many pieces as you like. That is why we packed 7 lightweight duffel bags instead of suitcases. The exact allowance depends on the Emirates fare you book, worth checking before booking.</p>

  <h3>Where does a family live right after arriving in Australia?</h3>
  <p>We used an Airbnb in Byron Bay as a bridge for the first three nights (765.70 euros for the whole booking of almost ten days, during which I already lived there alone). Then we moved into a house sit in the hinterland for four weeks, which costs no rent.</p>

  <h3>How cold is winter in and around Byron Bay?</h3>
  <p>In August the mean daily maximum at the Cape Byron station is 20.2 degrees Celsius, the minimum 13.2 degrees. Nights get chilly, colder in the hinterland, while days in the sun are pleasantly warm.</p>

  <h3>How quickly do kids settle in after emigrating?</h3>
  <p>Our two were wide awake and happy the moment they reached the beach on the first afternoon. After a week both seemed settled, but the honest part belongs in the answer too: it is early days, and much of it still feels like a holiday because school and work have not started yet.</p>

  <p><strong>Status:</strong> August 2026. Lucy and the kids have been here since 28 July, we are living in the hinterland house sit until the end of August. How things continue with housing and school, we will tell here step by step.</p>

